@startuml levenshtein-automaton
' How a Levenshtein query actually executes: the automaton for the query term W
' is SIMULATED (never materialized) while the dictionary trie is walked, so the
' two advance in lock-step — one trie edge consumed = one automaton transition.
' The transition is driven only by the characteristic vector, which is what makes
' a single, W-independent rule sufficient (Schulz & Mihov's "imitation" method).
' Labels use Unicode glyphs, not <latex>: house style, see docs/diagrams/README.md.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/levenshtein-automaton.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 2 in docs/integration/liblevenshtein/overview.md §4.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
skinparam NoteBorderColor #90A4AE
skinparam NoteFontColor #263238
title Levenshtein query — the automaton and the dictionary walk in lock-step

legend top right
  |= Color |= Concept |
  |<#1565C0>     | Automaton (query side) |
  |<#00838F>     | Dictionary trie (storage side) |
  |<#546E7A>     | Result / pruning |
endlegend

rectangle "**Query**\nterm W  (∣W∣ = m)\nmax edit distance k" as q #1565C0
rectangle "**Automaton state**  S\nreduced set of positions ⟨i, e⟩\ni = symbols of W consumed\ne = errors accumulated  (e ≤ k)\nS₀ = { ⟨0, 0⟩ }" as state #1565C0
rectangle "**Characteristic vector**  χ\nχ(x, W, i, k) ∈ { 0, 1 }^(2k+1)\nmarks where the trie symbol x\nmatches inside the window of W" as chi #1565C0
rectangle "**Transition**  δ(S, χ)\none fixed rule, independent of W;\nsubsumption ⊑ prunes dominated\npositions to keep ∣S∣ bounded" as delta #1565C0

rectangle "**Trie node**\nany ""D: Dictionary"" —\nDAWG · DoubleArray · ARTrie · PathMap" as node #00838F
rectangle "**Trie edge**\nsymbol x ∈ Σ\nunit = ""u8"" (byte) or ""char""" as edge #00838F

rectangle "**Accept**\nnode is final ∧ ∃ ⟨i, e⟩ ∈ S : i = ∣W∣\n⟹ **Candidate** { term, distance = e }" as accept #546E7A
rectangle "**Prune**\nS = ∅ ⟹ abandon the\nwhole subtree at once" as prune #546E7A

q --> state : initialize
state --> node : walk together
node --> edge : descend
edge --> chi : emit symbol x
chi --> delta
state --> delta
delta --> state : S′ = δ(S, χ)\n(one edge = one transition)
delta --> accept
delta --> prune

note right of delta
  δ depends ONLY on χ — never on the concrete symbols
  of W. One W-independent rule therefore drives *every*
  query, so the automaton is never constructed: it is
  **simulated** during the dictionary walk.
  — Schulz & Mihov (2002), the //imitation// method.
end note

note right of prune
  Pruning is what keeps the search sublinear in the
  dictionary: a subtree whose prefix already costs
  more than k edits is cut **once**, not re-tested
  once per term below it.
end note

note bottom of accept
  One candidate path costs 𝒪(m) transitions, each 𝒪(k) work on a
  state of bounded size — independent of the number of stored terms n.
  The distance e is returned with the term, so the caller can rank
  candidates without recomputing any edit distance.
end note

@enduml
